#When using variables, there are some rules when it comes to naming conventions
#Breaking these rules will disrupt your program and also make you an inefficient programer.
#These rules will make is easier for your code to be clean and easy to read:


#variable names can only contain letters, numbers, and underscores.
  #They CANNOT start with a number but can start with a underscore and letter.
  # ex. message_1 (Good) | 1_message (Bad)

#Spaces are NOT allowed to be used, you can use underscores in replacement of spaces.
  # greeting_message (Good) | greetingmessage (Bad)

#Avoid using python keywords, such as using function names as variable names.
  # ex. using print as a variable name.

#When trying to name a variable, try to make it short and clear.
  # ex. name (Good) | n (Bad) | student_name (Good) s_n (Bad)

 #Be careful when using lower and upper-case letters, because they can be confusing sometimes.
  # ex. A 0 (zero) can be mistaken for the letter O.

